  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH THURSDAY...Early afternoon satellite imagery together \nwith surface obs showed mostly clear skies across the region. A \nbroad area of surface high pressure was centered over the Great \nLakes, leading to light surface winds that were starting to take on a \neasterly direction. For the rest of today, expect dry conditions to \ncontinue. \n\nOvernight, the light winds will start to take on a southerly \ndirection as a upper level wave, with an accompanying weak surface \nlow and cold front, approach from the northwest. These southerly \nwinds should keep fog development at bay. After midnight, showers \nand thunderstorms are expected to move from North Dakota into \nnorthwest Minnesota. This should be north of a line from Alexandria \nto Lake Mille Lacs. \n\nBy early Thursday afternoon, light southerly winds together with \npartly sunny skies will lead to instability rebuilding across the \nregion ahead of the weak surface front. This will support scattered \nafternoon and evening thunderstorms. A few storms could produce \ngusty winds and large hail. SPC did upgrade the Marginal Risk to a \nSlight Risk for some locations, but honestly the overall threat was \nright on the marginal/slight line, so nothing really changed from a \nmeteorological standpoint. The lack of forcing, and relatively moist \nmid level lapse rates are some limiting factors for widespread \nthunderstorms. Storms will move southeast during the evening, and \nshould be out of the area by midnight.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY...No major changes from the previous \nforecast. The weak cold front will be through the area on Friday, \nmeaning dry conditions, but temperatures will remain seasonable with \nhighs in the low 80s.\n\nLooking ahead, these temperatures will continue this weekend and \ninto next week. From a big picture perspective, northwest flow will \nremain in place across the Upper Midwest. This is a dry pattern, as \nwe're never able to tap into and gulf moisture. That said, a few \nweak disturbances will be embedded in this northwest flow and move \nacross the region, brining periodic small chances for showers or \nthunderstorms. Most locations will stay dry. Monday night into \nTuesday will be the \"best\" chances for rain, but even then the \nprobability is only around 30 percent.",
